INT. TAXIDERMY SHOP - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

A cramped room hung with glass-eyed stags and mounted pheasants. A sleepover in sleeping bags on a threadbare rug. A single lantern.

Six girls, thirteen and fourteen, cross-legged in a ring. A STRANGER among them, a girl none of the others quite remembers inviting, sits at the edge of the lantern's reach, face half in shadow.

PRIYAMVADA "PRIYA" NAKASHIMA, 13, hugs a pillow. BRONWYN OKORO-LINDHOLM, 14, tells a story with a flashlight under her chin. SOFIA CALDERON-BEAUMONT, 13, braids her hair. IMANI THOMPSON-REYES, 14, flips through a magazine. And WILLA ACHTERBERG, 13, looks at the ceiling.

BRONWYN
...and the taxidermist never came home.

SOFIA
Stop.

BRONWYN
They found him in his own shop, stuffed.

PRIYA
That's gross.

IMANI
That's the plot of a movie.

BRONWYN
It's the plot of this shop.

WILLA
(quietly)
Someone is standing behind the deer.

Everyone looks. A stag's head hangs on the wall, its antlers throwing shadow. Nothing.

SOFIA
There's no one.

WILLA
I saw an eye.

IMANI
It's glass.

The lantern gutters. The Stranger leans forward. Her face enters the light: pale, calm, smiling.

STRANGER
Do you want to know my name?

PRIYA
We thought you came with Sofia.

SOFIA
I thought she came with you.

STRANGER
My name is Marguerite.

BRONWYN
(whispering)
Marguerite is the name on the shop door.

MARGUERITE
Yes.

She rises. The ring of girls draws back. The lantern flickers out.

In the dark, six pairs of eyes catch the last glint of the stag's glass stare. Then, one by one, they stop blinking.
